# abcmemory / abcremember vocabulary

## abcmemory

**abcmemory** means the committed agent guidance: `.cursor/skills/**`, `.cursor/rules/**`,
`.cursor/prompts/**`, `.cursor/hooks/**` and `.cursor/hooks.json`, `.cursorrules`, and
`.cursorignore`. `.llm/` is a planning workspace, not abcmemory; neither are `docs/` or `.github/`
unless the operator says so.

## abcremember

Create or update the best-fit abcmemory file, preferring an existing one, then follow it. Do not
write to `.llm/` unless asked. `/abcremember` or `/abcmemory`; process in
`.cursor/skills/abcmemory/SKILL.md`.

## next

**next** means "what next": answer with the operator's next step in the current task.

## abc* commands

Every abc command is a skill at `.cursor/skills/abc<name>/SKILL.md`. The operator runs it as
`/abc<name>` from the slash menu, which loads the skill. A plain `abc<name>` in a message,
resume text, or plan-runner prompt means the same thing: read that skill in full before acting.
A new command is a new skill whose `name` matches its folder, not a `.cursor/commands/` file.

These hold even before the skill is read:

- **abcplan** — never implement in that chat. Each set runs in a fresh worktree opened in its own
  Cursor window: lead with **Fresh worktree required**, then the create block, then the run block
  for the worktree's window (**git-worktree-sibling** § Plan sets in a worktree).
- **abcissue** — a pasted issue URL or path, or the word abcissue. Read the skill before
  planning. Do not implement in that chat, and do not post the tracker comment. A small set is a
  chat in the new worktree. A large set is **abcplan**.
- **abcdeploysteps** `<repo> <env>` — never guess either word. It writes a checklist and does
  not deploy.
- **abcdeployrun** `<repo> <env>` — never guess either word. It runs the unattended alpha server
  deploy. Read the skill before acting. Stop before any destructive action the operator did not
  ask for.
- **abcresume** — never run the resume commands; the operator picks one.
- **abccommitmsg** — draft a message for staged files, or for unstaged changes when nothing is
  staged. Do not commit.
- **abcagentoptimize** — during normal work, append ideas to its log; ask mid-turn only when the
  answer changes the current turn.
- **abcbranchesmerge** `<branch>…` — read the skill before acting. The agent names
  `feature/merge-YYYY-MM-DD` from `develop`. It authorizes merge commits and a non-force push
  of that branch only, and forbids deleting branches or worktrees.
- **abctriagereply** — read the skill before acting. The operator pasted a triage-bot handoff,
  or just finished checking one. Reply with the text they paste back into the triage bot.
  Do not change the cluster unless that same message asked for a change.

## legacy app

**v4 / OGen / Legacy** is current production: `podverse-api`, `podverse-rn`, and the other v4
checkouts in [01](/docs/proposals/v4-to-v5-migration/01-legacy-repos-and-reference-setup.md).
**legacy app** (also **podverse-rn**, **v4 mobile**) is the sibling checkout `../podverse-rn`.
**v5 / NextGen** (also written Nextgen) is this monorepo, including `apps/mobile`. See
**legacy-app-reference** and **v4-backend-reference**.

## livestreams

- A feed's medium (podcast, music, video, and so on) comes from the feed. Livestreams are a flag
  on top of that medium. A feed with live items is not always, or only, a livestream feed.
- Live items are item-level (`live_item`). Livestream links choose `/podcast/livestream/` or
  `/music/livestream/` by medium.
- Never model livestreams as a medium, a feed type, or an `AddByRSSResourceType` value. Regular
  and Add by RSS content follow the same rule.
